```cpp
// fragment - spiral order with four walls and the two re-tests
vector<int> spiral(const vector<vector<int>>& a) {
    vector<int> out;
    if (a.empty() || a[0].empty()) return out;
    int top = 0, bottom = (int)a.size() - 1, left = 0, right = (int)a[0].size() - 1;
    while (top <= bottom && left <= right) {
        for (int j = left; j <= right; ++j) out.push_back(a[top][j]);
        ++top;
        for (int i = top; i <= bottom; ++i) out.push_back(a[i][right]);
        --right;
        if (top <= bottom) { for (int j = right; j >= left; --j) out.push_back(a[bottom][j]); --bottom; }
        if (left <= right) { for (int i = bottom; i >= top; --i) out.push_back(a[i][left]); ++left; }
    }
    return out;
}
```

**Complexity.** Every traversal emits each of the $RC$ cells once: $\Theta(RC)$ time. The spiral keeps four integers and the diagonal loops two, so $O(1)$ extra space beyond the output. The shapes $1\times n$, $n\times 1$ and $1\times 1$ need no special code: the re-tests and the band bounds already cover them.

## A3. Transpose, rotate and reflect {#sec-mat-a3}

::: {.callout-note title="In plain words"}
A **transpose** flips a matrix across its main diagonal: row $i$, column $j$ goes to row $j$, column $i$. A **$90^\circ$ clockwise rotation** is a transpose followed by reversing every row. Both only *move* cells, so they are permutations of positions. On a square grid you can perform them in place with swaps; on a rectangle the shape changes ($R\times C$ becomes $C\times R$) and the easy route is a new grid.
:::

**Transpose of a square, in place.** Swap `M[i][j]` with `M[j][i]` for $i < j$ only. Each off-diagonal pair is exchanged once and the diagonal stays put; looping over all $i, j$ would swap every pair twice and change nothing.

::: {#a6cade27 .cell execution_count=13}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def transpose(M):
    n = len(M)
    assert all(len(r) == n for r in M)          # in place needs a square
    A = [row[:] for row in M]
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(i + 1, n):
            A[i][j], A[j][i] = A[j][i], A[i][j]
    return A


a3_M = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]
assert transpose(a3_M) == [[1, 4, 7], [2, 5, 8], [3, 6, 9]]
assert transpose(transpose(a3_M)) == a3_M       # an involution: doing it twice is the identity
```
:::


**Rotation as transpose + reverse.** Transposing sends $(i,j)$ to $(j,i)$; reversing each row then sends column $i$ to column $n-1-i$. Together $(i,j) \to (j, n-1-i)$, the clockwise quarter turn. Read the other way round, the rotated grid at $(i,j)$ holds
$$\text{rot}_{90}(M)[i][j] = M[n-1-j][i].$$ {#eq-mat-rot}

::: {#67a2922b .cell execution_count=14}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def rotate90(M):
    return [row[::-1] for row in transpose(M)]


def rotate180(M):
    return [row[::-1] for row in M][::-1]


def rotate270(M):
    return transpose(M)[::-1]                   # transpose, then reverse the ORDER of the rows


assert rotate90(a3_M) == [[7, 4, 1], [8, 5, 2], [9, 6, 3]]
assert rotate270(a3_M) == [[3, 6, 9], [2, 5, 8], [1, 4, 7]]
for _ in range(300):
    n = random.randint(1, 5)
    M = [[random.randint(0, 9) for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]
    R9 = rotate90(M)
    assert all(R9[i][j] == M[n - 1 - j][i] for i in range(n) for j in range(n))
    assert rotate90(rotate90(rotate90(rotate90(M)))) == M
    assert rotate180(M) == rotate90(rotate90(M))
    assert rotate270(M) == rotate90(rotate180(M))
```
:::


**The eight symmetries of a square.** Four rotations and four reflections map a square grid onto itself; together they form the *dihedral group* $D_4$. Each is one index formula and one cheap in-place recipe built from three moves: transpose, reverse each row, reverse the order of the rows.

| Transform | `new[i][j] =` | In place |
|---|---|---|
| identity | `M[i][j]` | nothing |
| rotate $90^\circ$ clockwise | `M[n-1-j][i]` | transpose, then reverse each row |
| rotate $180^\circ$ | `M[n-1-i][n-1-j]` | reverse row order, then reverse each row |
| rotate $90^\circ$ counter-clockwise | `M[j][n-1-i]` | transpose, then reverse row order |
| mirror left–right | `M[i][n-1-j]` | reverse each row |
| mirror top–bottom | `M[n-1-i][j]` | reverse row order |
| transpose (main diagonal) | `M[j][i]` | swap over $i<j$ |
| anti-transpose (other diagonal) | `M[n-1-j][n-1-i]` | transpose, then rotate $180^\circ$ |

::: {#cf4efa67 .cell execution_count=15}
``` {.python .cell-code}
D4 = {
    "id": lambda M, n, i, j: M[i][j],
    "rot90": lambda M, n, i, j: M[n - 1 - j][i],
    "rot180": lambda M, n, i, j: M[n - 1 - i][n - 1 - j],
    "rot270": lambda M, n, i, j: M[j][n - 1 - i],
    "mirror_lr": lambda M, n, i, j: M[i][n - 1 - j],
    "mirror_tb": lambda M, n, i, j: M[n - 1 - i][j],
    "transpose": lambda M, n, i, j: M[j][i],
    "anti": lambda M, n, i, j: M[n - 1 - j][n - 1 - i],
}


def apply(name, M):
    n = len(M)
    return [[D4[name](M, n, i, j) for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]


_M = [[4 * i + j for j in range(4)] for i in range(4)]
assert len({str(apply(k, _M)) for k in D4}) == 8                 # eight different grids
assert apply("rot90", _M) == rotate90(_M) and apply("rot270", _M) == rotate270(_M)
assert apply("mirror_lr", _M) == [r[::-1] for r in _M]
assert apply("mirror_tb", _M) == _M[::-1]
assert apply("anti", _M) == rotate180(transpose(_M))
assert apply("rot90", _M) == apply("transpose", apply("mirror_tb", _M))   # reverse rows first, then transpose
```
:::


The last line is worth remembering because it is the usual source of sign errors: *reverse the row order, then transpose* is also the clockwise turn, while *transpose, then reverse the row order* is counter-clockwise. When unsure, check the corner: clockwise sends the top-left value to the top-right.

**Rectangles.** For an $R\times C$ grid the clockwise turn is a $C\times R$ grid with `rot[j][R-1-i] = M[i][j]`. In Python that is `zip(*M[::-1])`: reverse the rows, then read columns.

::: {#55a6b855 .cell execution_count=16}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def rotate90_rect(M):
    return [list(col) for col in zip(*M[::-1])]


for _ in range(200):
    _r, _c = random.randint(1, 5), random.randint(1, 5)
    _A = [[random.randint(0, 9) for _ in range(_c)] for _ in range(_r)]
    _B = rotate90_rect(_A)
    assert len(_B) == _c and len(_B[0]) == _r
    assert all(_B[j][_r - 1 - i] == _A[i][j] for i in range(_r) for j in range(_c))
```
:::


**Why the in-place transpose is easy only for squares.** On a square, cell $(i,j)$ and cell $(j,i)$ simply trade places: the permutation is made of swaps. On an $R\times C$ grid stored flat, the value at flat index $p = iC + j$ must move to $q = jR + i$ in the $C\times R$ result. That is still a permutation of the same $RC$ slots, but its cycles can be long (for a $2\times 3$ grid one cycle is $1 \to 2 \to 4 \to 3 \to 1$), so in-place requires following cycles, as the juggling rotation of @sec-part-p5 does. Exercise 13 writes it out. Rotation inherits the same story through its transpose step.

**The four-cycle view.** A clockwise turn moves the four cells
$$(i,j) \to (j,\, n-1-i) \to (n-1-i,\, n-1-j) \to (n-1-j,\, i) \to (i,j)$$ {#eq-mat-orbit}
around one **orbit**. Every cell is in exactly one orbit of four, except the centre of an odd grid, which is an orbit of one. So a rotation can also be done by visiting one representative per orbit and moving its four values one step, with one temporary — no transpose at all (Rotate Image, @sec-mat-p2-rotate). Orbits matter beyond rotation: a grid that looks the same after a quarter turn is exactly a grid that is constant on every orbit (Mirror Grid, @sec-mat-p2-mirror).

