// fragment - max-sum sub-rectangle: fix (top, bottom), fold columns, Kadane
long long maxSubmatrix(const vector<vector<int>>& a) {
    int R = (int)a.size(), C = (int)a[0].size();
    long long best = LLONG_MIN;
    for (int top = 0; top < R; ++top) {
        vector<long long> col(C, 0);
        for (int bottom = top; bottom < R; ++bottom) {
            for (int j = 0; j < C; ++j) col[j] += a[bottom][j];
            long long cur = col[0], run = col[0];      // Kadane over the column sums
            for (int j = 1; j < C; ++j) {
                cur = max(col[j], cur + col[j]);
                run = max(run, cur);
            }
            best = max(best, run);
        }
    }
    return best;
}
```

**Complexity.** $O(R^2)$ bands, each folded and scanned in $O(C)$ (times the kernel's own cost): $O(R^2C)$ time and $O(C)$ extra space for Kadane, the hash map and the pair count. The four-loop search it replaces is $O(R^2C^2)$ even with $O(1)$ rectangle sums.

## A6. Coordinate invariants and parity {#sec-mat-a6}

::: {.callout-note title="In plain words"}
An **invariant** is a number that a move or an operation cannot change. On a grid the useful ones are simple functions of the coordinates: $i + j$, $i - j$, their parity, $(j - i) \bmod n$. Once you know what a move preserves, you often know the answer without simulating anything — or you know it is impossible.
:::

**Diagonal keys.** A step down-right $(+1,+1)$ keeps $i - j$; a step down-left $(+1,-1)$ keeps $i + j$. So a chess bishop never leaves the diagonal and anti-diagonal through its square, and "sum of everything a bishop attacks" is one diagonal sum plus one anti-diagonal sum minus the shared cell — precompute a sum per key and answer each cell in $O(1)$. The same keys drive the diagonal traversals of @sec-mat-a2 and the two diagonals of Matrix Diagonal Sum (@sec-mat-p5-diagsum).

**Checkerboard parity.** Colour cell $(i,j)$ by $(i + j) \bmod 2$. A step to a side neighbour changes $i + j$ by exactly one, so it **always changes the colour**. Three consequences:

- Any walk of side steps from $(i_1,j_1)$ to $(i_2,j_2)$ has length with the parity of $(i_1+j_1) - (i_2+j_2)$. A walk that returns to its start has even length.
- A domino (two side-adjacent cells) always covers one cell of each colour. The classic *mutilated chessboard* — an $8\times 8$ board with two opposite corners removed — has $30$ cells of one colour and $32$ of the other, so it cannot be tiled by dominoes, however you try.
- Side-adjacent cells can be forced to differ by making every value's parity equal to its cell's colour (Engineer Artem, @sec-mat-p5-artem).

::: {#62643534 .cell execution_count=26}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def colour(i, j):
    return (i + j) % 2


for _ in range(300):                                   # random side-step walks
    i = j = 0
    steps = random.randint(0, 30)
    for _s in range(steps):
        di, dj = random.choice(DIRS4)
        i, j = i + di, j + dj
    assert (steps - (i + j)) % 2 == 0                  # length has the parity of the key change

_board = [(i, j) for i in range(8) for j in range(8) if (i, j) not in [(0, 0), (7, 7)]]
assert sum(colour(i, j) == 0 for i, j in _board) == 30
assert sum(colour(i, j) == 1 for i, j in _board) == 32  # unequal colours: no domino tiling
```
:::


**Invariants of an operation.** The same reasoning works for operations on values. Suppose one operation adds $1$ to two side-adjacent cells. It adds $1$ to one cell of each colour, so
$$D = \sum_{\text{colour } 0} a_{ij} - \sum_{\text{colour } 1} a_{ij}$$
never changes. If the target grid has a different $D$, no sequence of operations reaches it — a one-line impossibility proof that no search could give.

::: {#85129fb3 .cell execution_count=27}
