:::


**Wrapped diagonals.** If all rows and columns of an $n\times n$ grid are shifted cyclically together (the grid is a torus), cell $(i,j)$ moves to $((i+a) \bmod n,\, (j+b) \bmod n)$, and its key $(j - i) \bmod n$ changes by the constant $b - a$ for *every* cell. So the $n$ wrapped diagonals move as whole blocks and their contents never mix — the key to Matrix and Shifts (@sec-mat-p5-shifts).

**Rotation orbits.** A quarter turn splits a square grid into orbits of four cells (@eq-mat-orbit), plus the centre when $n$ is odd. A grid unchanged by the turn is exactly a grid constant on each orbit, so "make it symmetric" problems decide each orbit independently (Mirror Grid, @sec-mat-p2-mirror). Whether a centre exists is decided by the **parity of $n$** — the same parity that decides whether the two diagonals of a square share a cell.

**Rotating the coordinates: Manhattan becomes Chebyshev.** Put $u = i + j$ and $v = i - j$. Then
$$|\Delta i| + |\Delta j| = \max(|\Delta u|, |\Delta v|).$$ {#eq-mat-uv}
Manhattan distance (side steps) turns into Chebyshev distance (king moves) in the rotated coordinates, where the two axes separate. "The farthest point in Manhattan distance" becomes "the extreme values of $u$ and $v$", four numbers maintained in $O(1)$ each.

