##### C++

```cpp
vector<int> spiralRing(vector<vector<int>>& M) {
    vector<int> out;
    if (M.empty() || M[0].empty()) return out;
    int R = M.size(), C = M[0].size();
    for (int k = 0; k < (min(R, C) + 1) / 2; ++k) {
        int top = k, bottom = R - 1 - k, left = k, right = C - 1 - k;
        for (int j = left; j <= right; ++j) out.push_back(M[top][j]);
        for (int i = top + 1; i <= bottom; ++i) out.push_back(M[i][right]);
        if (top < bottom)
            for (int j = right - 1; j >= left; --j) out.push_back(M[bottom][j]);
        if (left < right)
            for (int i = bottom - 1; i > top; --i) out.push_back(M[i][left]);
    }
    return out;
}
```

:::

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
All three rungs are $\Theta(mn)$ time and emit every cell once. **Direction walk** uses $\Theta(mn)$ auxiliary space for the `seen` grid; **shrinking walls** and **ring peel** use $\Theta(1)$ auxiliary space. At $m,n \le 10$ the difference is invisible, but the $O(1)$-space forms are the ones that generalise to grid problems where the visited set is too large to keep.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- A single row `[[1,2,3]]` and a single column `[[1],[2],[3]]` both spiral to `[1,2,3]`; only the two guards (or the `top < bottom` / `left < right` tests) prevent a double read.
- A $1 \times 1$ matrix returns its one element. An empty matrix returns `[]` — guarded at the top of every rung.
- A rectangle where one dimension is $2$ (e.g. $2 \times 3$) has no interior ring, so the loop count `(min(R,C)+1)//2` is exactly $1$.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Forgetting the guards before the bottom and left sides.** After folding `top` past `bottom`, a bare bottom-row loop re-reads the top row in reverse. The two `if` tests are load-bearing, not cosmetic.
- **`right - 1` vs `right` on the bottom edge of the ring peel.** The ring's right column already emitted the bottom-right corner, so the ring's bottom edge starts at `right - 1`; starting at `right` duplicates the corner. (The shrinking-walls form avoids this by decrementing `right` before the bottom loop.)
- **Rotating the direction more than once per step.** A single clockwise turn always suffices on a rectangular grid; a `while` around the turn suggests the head is stuck inside a fully-seen region.
:::

**Variants.** [LC 59 · Spiral Matrix II](https://leetcode.com/problems/spiral-matrix-ii/){difficulty="Medium"} fills an $n \times n$ grid with $1..n^2$ along the same spiral — reuse the shrinking walls and write instead of read. [LC 885 · Spiral Matrix III](https://leetcode.com/problems/spiral-matrix-iii/){difficulty="Medium"} starts at an arbitrary cell and spirals outward past the grid's edges, which only the direction walk handles naturally. Diagonal Traverse (@sec-mat-p1-diag) keeps "walk, then turn at a border" but rides diagonals instead of edges.

**Try it.** Return the spiral as a list of rings (the $3 \times 3$ sample gives `[1,2,3,6,9,8,7,4]` and `[5]`). Then spiral from the centre outward: one valid outward order of the $3 \times 3$ is `[5,6,9,8,7,4,1,2,3]`.

