## Pattern 3: In-place marking — the matrix keeps its own notes {#sec-mat-p3}

**From moving cells to writing on them.** P2's cells kept their values and changed places. Here cells keep their places but must carry extra information while a pass is still reading them: "this row is doomed", "this cell was alive". With no room for a second grid, the notes go into storage the matrix does not need (@sec-mat-a4).

**Recognise it.** "In place", "$O(1)$ extra space", "update all cells simultaneously", and a rule where one cell's new value depends on *old* values elsewhere.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
1. **Find spare storage:** the first row and column (one flag per line), spare high bits of each cell (one extra state per cell), or impossible values such as negatives.
2. **Record pass:** compute every note from *original* values only; save anything the notes will overwrite (the `row0` / `col0` booleans).
3. **Act pass:** apply the notes, reading each note before anything overwrites it; restore or decode last.

**Invariant:** until the act pass, the original value of every cell is still recoverable.
:::

**Complexity.** $\Theta(RC)$ time per round, $O(1)$ extra space.

### Problem: Set matrix zeroes {#sec-mat-p3-zeroes}

::: {.p-statement}
Given an $m \times n$ matrix, if an element is $0$, set its entire row and column to $0$. Do it **in place**.

$1 \le m, n \le 200$, cells are integers. `[[1,1,1],[1,0,1],[1,1,1]]` becomes `[[1,0,1],[0,0,0],[1,0,1]]`. `[[0,1,2,0],[3,4,5,2],[1,3,1,5]]` becomes `[[0,0,0,0],[0,4,5,0],[0,3,1,0]]`.

Source: [LC 73 · Set Matrix Zeroes](https://leetcode.com/problems/set-matrix-zeroes/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Wiping a row the moment you see a zero creates zeros that were not in the input, and reading those later dooms rows and columns that should survive. So **decide first, wipe second**: record which rows and columns must go from the original values, then wipe — and to do it in $O(1)$ space, keep that record in row 0 and column 0 of the matrix itself.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Clone and repaint.** Copy the matrix, find the doomed rows and columns in the copy (which never changes while you read it), and repaint the original. $O(mn)$ extra memory.
2. **Two boolean arrays.** You only need *which lines* are doomed, not the whole grid: `rows[i]` and `cols[j]`, set in pass one, read in pass two. $O(m + n)$ extra memory — the standard interview answer.
3. **Header marking.** Use row 0 as the column flags and column 0 as the row flags (@sec-mat-a4). First save two booleans saying whether row 0 and column 0 themselves contain a zero; then mark from the interior, wipe the interior, and apply the two booleans last. $O(1)$ extra memory.

**Invariant.** Every flag is written from an original value: the interior is never wiped before all marks are placed, and row 0 / column 0 are never wiped before the interior has read them.
:::

