::: {.p-complexity}
All three are $\Theta(mn)$ time. Extra space drops along the ladder: **Clone and repaint** $O(mn)$, **Two boolean arrays** $O(m+n)$, **Header marking** $O(1)$. The $O(1)$ version is the one the problem's follow-up asks for, and the one most often implemented wrong because of the saved-flag ordering.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- Every cell zero: the result is all zeros; the two saved flags both fire and row 0 / col 0 are wiped last.
- A single zero in the corner dooms one row and one column only; the other $m+n-2$ lines are untouched.
- $1 \times n$ or $m \times 1$: row 0 *is* the interior, so the header form's loops from index $1$ correctly do nothing and rely on `zfr` / `zfc` alone.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Wiping while scanning.** Setting a whole row to zero as soon as you see a zero invents zeros that were never in the input; their columns then get wrongly doomed. Always finish all marks before any wipe.
- **Forgetting the two saved flags.** In the $O(1)$ form, row 0 and col 0 double as flag storage, so their *own* doom must be recorded before the marking pass overwrites them; otherwise the first row/col never gets cleared (or gets cleared spuriously).
- **Wiping row 0 / col 0 too early.** They must be zeroed *after* the interior wipe, or the interior wipe reads a flag you already destroyed.
:::

**Variants.** Game of Life (@sec-mat-p3-life) needs the old value of *every* cell, not one bit per line, and finds the spare storage in an unused bit of each cell. The boolean-array form is the template for any "flag the lines, then apply" grid problem; [CF 486B · OR in Matrix](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/486/B){rating="1300"} runs it backwards — given the OR of each row and column, recover a matrix by marking the lines that must be zero.

**Try it.** Make the header form use a *single* extra boolean: keep row 0's flag in `a[0][0]` and a separate `col0`, and wipe from the bottom-right corner upward so `a[0][0]` is read before it is overwritten (exercise 14). Verify against `zeroes_copy` on random grids.

