::: {.p-complexity}
**Copy the board**: $O(mn)$ time (eight neighbours per cell), $O(mn)$ extra memory. **Two states in one cell**: the same $O(mn)$ time, $O(1)$ extra memory — the second pass costs one more sweep, not a different order of growth.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- A $1 \times 1$ board has no neighbours, so a live cell dies and a dead one stays dead.
- Border and corner cells have only five or three neighbours; clamp the neighbour ranges (`max(0, i-1)` … `min(m, i+2)`) instead of padding the board.
- A board where nothing changes (a still life such as the $2 \times 2$ block of live cells) must come back identical: every live cell becomes `3`, then `1`.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Counting `board[x][y]` instead of `board[x][y] & 1`.** A neighbour already updated to `2` or `3` is then counted as two live cells, or a dead-now cell as live.
- **Writing the final value in pass 1.** Setting `board[i][j] = new` destroys the old state that cells to the right and below still need — the same cascade as wiping a row too early in Set Matrix Zeroes.
:::

**Variants.** The follow-up "what if the board is infinite?" changes the representation, not the rule: store only the live cells in a set and count neighbours with a hash map. [CF 1864D · Matrix Cascade](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1864/D){rating="1700"} is another "decide every cell from marks left by earlier cells" sweep: a flip at $(i,j)$ affects a widening cone below it, and you carry the pending flips down the rows instead of applying them cell by cell.

