## Pattern 5: Matrix invariants and parity — read the answer off the coordinates {#sec-mat-p5}

**From collapsing a dimension to not walking at all.** P4 still looked at every band. Some grid questions need no traversal of candidates at all: the answer is a statement about coordinates — a diagonal is $i = j$ or $i + j = n-1$, a distance is $|r - r_0| + |c - c_0|$, a free shift preserves $(j - i) \bmod n$, a side step flips $(i + j) \bmod 2$ (@sec-mat-a6). Find the invariant, evaluate it once, and handle the one place it double-counts or breaks.

**Recognise it.** Free operations ("any number of shifts / rotations at no cost"), "minimum number of moves" on an empty grid, "adjacent cells must differ", "is it possible", a fixed small grid, or symmetric structures where the parity of $n$ matters.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
1. Ask what a move or operation **cannot change**: $i+j$, $i-j$, $(i+j) \bmod 2$, $(j-i) \bmod n$, the orbit of a cell, a weighted sum of values.
2. Group the cells by that quantity; the problem usually splits into independent groups.
3. Solve each group by a formula (majority, count, distance), and watch for the **overlap** two identities both claim — whose existence is often decided by the parity of a dimension.
:::

**Complexity.** One $O(RC)$ pass, often less; the invariant replaces a search.

### Problem: Matrix diagonal sum {#sec-mat-p5-diagsum}

::: {.p-statement}
Given an $n \times n$ matrix, return the sum of the two diagonals: the main diagonal ($i = j$) and the anti-diagonal ($i + j = n - 1$). A cell that lies on both — the exact centre when $n$ is odd — is counted only once.

$1 \le n \le 100$, $1 \le$ cell $\le 100$. `[[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7,8,9]]` gives $1+5+9 + 3+5+7 - 5 = 25$. A $4 \times 4$ of all ones gives $8$ (no shared cell, because $n$ is even).

Source: [LC 1572 · Matrix Diagonal Sum](https://leetcode.com/problems/matrix-diagonal-sum/){.solved difficulty="Easy"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Each diagonal is a one-line relation on the indices — the main diagonal is $i = j$, the anti-diagonal is $i + j = n - 1$ — so row $i$ contributes exactly $a[i][i]$ and $a[i][n-1-i]$. The only trap is parity: when $n$ is odd the centre satisfies both relations and must be counted once, when $n$ is even the diagonals never meet.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Two passes, subtract the centre.** Sum each diagonal with its own loop, then subtract $a[n/2][n/2]$ when $n$ is odd.
2. **One loop.** Both diagonals are indexed by the same row $i$, so add `a[i][i] + a[i][n-1-i]` per row and correct the odd centre once at the end.
3. **Guarded single pass.** Add the anti-diagonal cell only when it is not the main-diagonal cell, $n - 1 - i \ne i$; the overlap is never added, so nothing is corrected.

**Invariant.** The two diagonals meet in the cells with $i = j$ and $i + j = n - 1$, i.e. $2i = n - 1$: no cell when $n$ is even, exactly the centre when $n$ is odd (@sec-mat-a6).
:::

