::: {.p-complexity}
**Two passes, subtract the centre**, **One loop** and **Guarded single pass** are all $\Theta(n)$ time and $O(1)$ space: they read only the $2n - (n \bmod 2)$ diagonal cells, never the other $n^2 - 2n$ cells.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- $n = 1$: the single cell is both diagonals; the guarded form adds it once, the subtract forms add-then-remove. All return the cell.
- Even $n$: there is no centre, `overlap == 0`, and the guarded condition `n-1-i != i` is always true, so the two strategies agree with no correction.
- Anti-diagonal index is $n-1-i$, not $n-i$; a $+1$ reads one column off the right edge.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Forgetting the odd-$n$ centre.** Returning `main + anti` gives $30$ on the first sample instead of $25$; the centre $5$ is counted twice.
- **Subtracting on even $n$ too.** `n % 2` must gate the correction; subtracting `a[n//2][n//2]` on a $4 \times 4$ of ones yields $7$ instead of $8$.
- **Off-by-one on the anti index.** `n - 1 - i` is the last column of row $i$; `n - i` indexes past it.
:::

**Variants.** Beautiful Matrix (@sec-mat-p5-beautiful) shares the coordinate lens: one cell is judged by its row and column distance to the centre. [LC 766 · Toeplitz Matrix](https://leetcode.com/problems/toeplitz-matrix/){difficulty="Easy"} asks whether every $i - j$ diagonal is constant, which is a single comparison $a[i][j] = a[i-1][j-1]$ per cell. Summing the boundary ring is the same "only touch the cells the relation selects" idea, $O(n)$ instead of $O(n^2)$.

