### Problem: Beautiful matrix {#sec-mat-p5-beautiful}

::: {.p-statement}
A $5 \times 5$ matrix holds twenty-four $0$s and a single $1$. Rows and columns are indexed $1..5$. In one move you may slide the $1$ one cell up, down, left, or right into a neighbouring cell. Find the minimum number of moves that bring the $1$ to the centre, position $(3, 3)$.

Input is five lines of five integers. When the $1$ is on the **second row, fifth column** the answer is $3$.

Source: [CF 263A · Beautiful Matrix](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/263/A){.solved rating="800"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
The $1$ moves one step at a time on an empty grid, so the fewest moves from $(r, c)$ to the centre $(3, 3)$ is the **Manhattan distance** $|r - 3| + |c - 3|$: each move closes the row gap or the column gap by one, and the two gaps are independent. The problem is only "find the one non-zero cell", then a formula.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Locate, then measure.** One row-major scan records the position of the $1$; the answer is $|r-3| + |c-3|$ (1-indexed).
2. **Single nested scan.** Apply the formula the moment the scan reads the $1$; there is exactly one, so that value is final.
3. **Answer while reading input.** Never store the grid: read the 25 numbers and print the formula for the one that is $1$.

**Invariant.** Every move changes $|r - 3| + |c - 3|$ by exactly one, so at least that many moves are needed, and moving straight along the row and then along the column achieves it.
:::

