::: {.p-edge}
- The $1$ already at the centre $(3,3)$ gives $0$ moves.
- The four corners are the farthest cells at distance $4$; the answer range is $0 \dots 4$.
- 0-indexed coordinates use centre $2$, 1-indexed use centre $3$; the two formulas differ only by that constant, and mixing them is the sole way to be wrong.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- **Index-base mismatch.** If you index rows $0..4$ but subtract $3$, every answer is shifted; use $|i-2|+|j-2|$ for 0-indexed or $|r-3|+|c-3|$ for 1-indexed, never a blend.
- **Chebyshev instead of Manhattan.** A move changes one coordinate by one, so distance is $|dr| + |dc|$, not $\max(|dr|, |dc|)$; diagonal moves are not offered.
- **Storing the whole grid when you only need one coordinate** — harmless at $5 \times 5$, but the streaming rung exists precisely because the rest of the matrix is irrelevant.
:::

**Variants.** Matrix Diagonal Sum (@sec-mat-p5-diagsum) is the same "a coordinate relation picks the interesting cells" idea. [CF 1689D · Lena and Matrix](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1689/D){rating="1900"} is the hard version of "Manhattan distance on a grid": find the cell minimising the largest Manhattan distance to a set of black cells, made easy by the rotation $u = i + j$, $v = i - j$ of @sec-mat-a6, which turns Manhattan distance into $\max(|\Delta u|, |\Delta v|)$.

**Try it.** Generalise to an $n \times n$ grid with $n$ odd: the 0-indexed centre is $(\lfloor n/2 \rfloor, \lfloor n/2 \rfloor)$, so a $1$ in a corner of the $3 \times 3$ grid needs $2$ moves. If diagonal moves were allowed too, the answer would be the Chebyshev distance $\max(|\Delta r|, |\Delta c|)$, and the first sample would drop to $2$.

