[**From a fixed coordinate to an invariant of free moves.** Beautiful Matrix measured one cell. Matrix and Shifts allows unlimited free shifts, so no single position matters — only the quantity a shift cannot change, the wrapped diagonal $(j - i) \bmod n$.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Matrix and shifts {#sec-mat-p5-shifts}

::: {.p-statement}
You are given an $n \times n$ binary matrix. First you may, for free and as often as you like, cyclically shift **all rows** up or down by one, or **all columns** left or right by one. Then you may flip single cells, each flip costing $1$. Find the minimum cost to turn the matrix into the identity matrix (ones exactly on the main diagonal).

$1 \le t \le 10^4$, $1 \le n \le 2000$, and the sum of $n^2$ over all tests is at most $4 \cdot 10^6$. Samples: `010 / 011 / 100` costs $1$; `10 / 10` costs $2$; the $4 \times 4$ matrix of ones with a single `0` at row 2, column 2 costs $11$.

Source: [CF 1660E · Matrix and Shifts](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/1660/E){.solved rating="1600"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
A free shift moves every cell by the same offset on a torus, so it never changes which cells share a **wrapped diagonal** $(j - i) \bmod n$ — it only decides which wrapped diagonal lands on the main one. Pick the wrapped diagonal with the most ones, $\textit{best}$: the cost is $(n - \textit{best})$ flips to fill it plus $(\textit{total} - \textit{best})$ flips to clear every other one.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
1. **Try every shift.** Shifting rows by $a$ and columns by $b$ only matters through $a - b \bmod n$, so try the $n$ column shifts with no row shift. For each, count the cells that disagree with the identity: $O(n^2)$ per shift, $O(n^3)$ in all.
2. **Count wrapped diagonals once.** One pass adds each `1` at $(i,j)$ to `cnt[(j - i) mod n]`. A shift that puts diagonal $d$ on the main diagonal costs $(n - \text{cnt}[d]) + (\textit{total} - \text{cnt}[d])$, so take the largest count. $O(n^2)$.

**Invariant.** Under a shift by $(a, b)$ cell $(i,j)$ goes to $(i+a, j+b) \bmod n$, and $(j+b) - (i+a) \equiv (j - i) + (b - a)$: every cell's diagonal index moves by the same constant. So the $n$ wrapped diagonals are permuted as whole blocks, and their sizes and contents are invariant (@sec-mat-a6).
:::

