**17. [E] A token walks by side steps (up, down, left, right) from $(0,0)$ to $(3,4)$, possibly revisiting cells. Can the walk have exactly $10$ steps? $11$?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
The colour $(i+j) \bmod 2$ flips on every side step, and $(0+0)$ and $(3+4)$ differ in parity, so every walk has odd length. $11$ is possible (the shortest walk has $7$ steps, and a detour forward and back adds $2$ each time); $10$ never is (@sec-mat-a6).
:::

**18. [M] Can a $6\times 6$ board with the two cells $(0,0)$ and $(5,5)$ removed be tiled by $1\times 2$ dominoes? What about removing $(0,0)$ and $(0,5)$?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$(0,0)$ and $(5,5)$ have $i+j = 0$ and $10$, both even, so the board loses two cells of one colour: $16$ against $18$. Each domino covers one of each colour, so no tiling exists. $(0,0)$ and $(0,5)$ have different colours, so the counts are $17$ and $17$ — the colouring argument no longer forbids a tiling, and one exists: row 0 keeps cells $1..4$, two horizontal dominoes; rows $1..5$ are a full $5\times 6$ block, three horizontal dominoes per row. Equal colour counts are necessary for a tiling but not sufficient in general, so when they match you still have to exhibit one (@sec-mat-a6).
:::

**19. [M] Given $p$ points on a grid, find for each query point the largest Manhattan distance to any of them in $O(1)$ after $O(p)$ preprocessing.**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Store $\min$ and $\max$ of $u = i+j$ and of $v = i - j$ over the points. By @eq-mat-uv the farthest distance from $(i,j)$ is $\max(u_q - u_{\min},\; u_{\max} - u_q,\; v_q - v_{\min},\; v_{\max} - v_q)$ with $u_q = i+j$, $v_q = i-j$. The cell in @sec-mat-a6 checks this against brute force. CF 1689D (Lena and Matrix) is this idea applied to every cell of a grid.
:::

**20. [H] One operation picks a $2\times 2$ block and adds $1$ to all four cells. Which quantity is invariant, and when is a target grid reachable from the all-zero grid on an $R\times C$ board?**

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Every $2\times 2$ block contains two cells of each colour, so the colour difference $D$ of @sec-mat-a6 stays $0$: a target with $D \ne 0$ is unreachable. That is necessary, not sufficient. To decide exactly, notice that the operations are *forced*. Visit the block positions $(i,j)$, $i \le R-2$, $j \le C-2$, in row-major order. Cell $(i,j)$ is covered only by the blocks at $(i-1,j-1)$, $(i-1,j)$, $(i,j-1)$ and $(i,j)$, and the first three are already decided, so block $(i,j)$ must be used exactly "target minus what is already there" times. The target is reachable exactly when every forced count is non-negative and, at the end, the last row and the last column match too. $O(RC)$ time. Invariants prune quickly; the forced sweep decides.

::: {#2dd06df6 .cell execution_count=104}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def reachable_2x2(T):
    R, C = len(T), len(T[0])
    cur = [[0] * C for _ in range(R)]
    for i in range(R - 1):
        for j in range(C - 1):
            k = T[i][j] - cur[i][j]
            if k < 0:
                return False
            for a, b in ((i, j), (i, j + 1), (i + 1, j), (i + 1, j + 1)):
                cur[a][b] += k
    return cur == T


def reachable_brute(T, limit=3):
    R, C = len(T), len(T[0])
    blocks = [(i, j) for i in range(R - 1) for j in range(C - 1)]
    def build(counts):
        g = [[0] * C for _ in range(R)]
        for (i, j), k in zip(blocks, counts):
            for a, b in ((i, j), (i, j + 1), (i + 1, j), (i + 1, j + 1)):
                g[a][b] += k
        return g
    from itertools import product
    return any(build(cs) == T for cs in product(range(limit + 1), repeat=len(blocks)))


for _ in range(200):
    _r, _c = random.randint(2, 3), random.randint(2, 3)
    _T = [[random.randint(0, 2) for _ in range(_c)] for _ in range(_r)]
    assert reachable_2x2(_T) == reachable_brute(_T)
    if reachable_2x2(_T):
        assert colour_diff(_T) == 0                      # the invariant is necessary
```
:::


