## Further reading

- [Wikipedia — Row- and column-major order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Row-_and_column-major_order) — the address arithmetic of @sec-mat-a1, its generalisation to more dimensions, and which languages use which order.
- [Wikipedia — In-place matrix transposition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In-place_matrix_transposition) — the cycle structure of the rectangular transpose (exercise 13) and the cache-friendly algorithms for it.
- [Wikipedia — Maximum subarray problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximum_subarray_problem) — Kadane's algorithm, the 1-D kernel of @sec-mat-a5, and its 2-D version.
- [CP-Algorithms — Finding the largest zero submatrix](https://cp-algorithms.com/dynamic_programming/zero_matrix.html) — the other way to search rectangles: fix the bottom row and solve a histogram with a stack in $O(RC)$.
- [Wikipedia — Mutilated chessboard problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mutilated_chessboard_problem) — the checkerboard-colouring invariant of @sec-mat-a6, its history, and Gomory's theorem on when tilings do exist.
- [Wikipedia — Chebyshev distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev_distance) — the $45^\circ$ rotation that turns Manhattan distance into Chebyshev distance (@eq-mat-uv).
- [Wikipedia — Dihedral group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dihedral_group) — the eight symmetries of a square from @sec-mat-a3 as a group, and why rotations and reflections compose the way they do.
- [Wikipedia — Conway's Game of Life](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conway%27s_Game_of_Life) — the rules behind @sec-mat-p3-life, still lifes and oscillators to test an implementation on.
